+ * So row 1 prints the derivation as **what the next rebalance will produce** rather than as a
+ * figure the pot must already equal, and fails on the one relationship that genuinely cannot be
+ * legitimate: **the pot exceeding its own opening allocation while a commitment is outstanding.**
+ * That can only happen if a refund credited `transferBudget` with more than it took — which is
+ * precisely the failure mode the 2026-11-20 refund introduces, so it is the right thing to guard.
+ *
+ * Row 2 is the refund's own invariant, and it is an **INEQUALITY**: entries are permission slips
+ * against the total, so they may account for less than it holds but never more. When they claim
+ * more, a refund is over-refunded and floored — what `reportDofOverRefund` warns about, and that
+ * warning is dev-builds only, so this is the one place it is visible on demand.
+ *
+ * ⚠️ **It is NOT `entries sum = committed`, and that guess is wrong in a way no test would catch
+ * until somebody had been sold.** A refund retires the whole entry but gives back only the weeks
+ * AFTER the departure, so the weeks the club actually used stay on the total with no entry behind
+ * them. That remainder is correct and permanent for the period.
+ *
+ * The non-refundable remainder therefore has two legitimate sources and one illegitimate one, and
+ * the row reports the figure instead of judging it: **used weeks of departed contracts**
+ * (correct), a **charge drawn with no `subjectId`** (carried correctly but never refundable — the
+ * one way this mechanism is incomplete rather than wrong), and a genuine leak. A remainder that
+ * is large and growing on a save with few departures points at the middle one.
